<p>President Donald Trump warned the UK not to “give away” Diego Garcia, the joint US-UK military base on the Chagos Islands in the Indian Ocean. He criticized Prime Minister Keir Starmer’s agreement with Mauritius, which would transfer sovereignty while leasing the base back to Britain for roughly a century. Trump’s comments came soon after the US State Department again signaled support for the UK-Mauritius deal, leaving a mixed message between the White House and the administration’s diplomatic line. Trump linked his objection to broader security concerns, saying the US might need Diego Garcia—along with an airfield at Fairford in England—in connection with possible action involving Iran, including the possibility of strikes if Iran “decide not to make a Deal”. The UK government defended the arrangement as the best way to secure the base’s long-term future, arguing it is the “only way” to guarantee the facility can keep operating with the US.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/TGVPEf3agU6HPwMoTy9ibWDsQZeaJaXDe7XEATLJpPvr1LY-Yrl-GAzjMUVTEV576UGRSB7lxKgnG-78sMC4Lzx9d0LdkWyl-KTD9G5XHJsEnnBUpepbLnFuZl88i0Lpm6qZRCyUAAkkYXXI4d3FC72fQgwY9VA6vnUweYb7v34n_bdtjo9k4gdtDal9w78xxlRvYJ2NlZrmZiBICrow-XwBWsd6_BEJFhFjK06mIaC_rw' alt='An undated file photo shows Diego Garcia, the largest island in the Chagos archipelago and site of a major US military base in the middle of the Indian Ocean, which the US leased from Britain in 1966.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>What Trump objected to: Trump said “Leases are no good when it comes to Countries,” calling the proposed arrangement a “tenuous” long lease (often described as a 99-year deal in reporting) and urging Starmer not to “give away” Diego Garcia.</li><li>Policy whiplash context: This is not the first time Trump has attacked a deal that US officials backed. Coverage pointed to an earlier episode in January after formal US support in 2025; the State Department then signaled backing again shortly before Trump’s latest post.</li><li>China concerns: The South China Morning Post said Trump also warned about strategic vulnerabilities, pointing to concerns about China’s growing ties with Mauritius as part of the backdrop.</li><li>Tone and messaging: On Truth Social, Trump cast the dispute as a test of UK resolve and alliance politics. He warned that losing Diego Garcia would be a “blight on our Great Ally” and told Britain to stay strong “in the face of Wokeism”.</li></ul><blockquote>Prime Minister Starmer should not lose control, for any reason, of Diego Garcia, by entering a tenuous, at best, 100 Year Lease - President Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>US State Department: US officials reiterated support for the UK-Mauritius agreement even as Trump criticized it publicly. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c309qqyer8yo'>BBC News</a>)</li><li>UK government: London defended the deal as the “only way” to secure the long-term future of the joint US-UK base at Diego Garcia. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/trump-flips-position-on-uk-s-chagos-islands-deal-approval/a-76028349'>DW</a>)</li><li>Mauritius government: Mauritius has long argued the UK forced it to give up the Chagos Islands in the 1960s to make way for the base, and that it would regain sovereignty under the deal. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/donald-trump-blasts-keir-starmer-diego-garcia-deal-hours-chagos-after-us-backed-it/'>POLITICO Europe</a>)</li><li>President Donald Trump: Trump said the base could be needed if Iran does not “make a Deal,” framing Diego Garcia as central to deterring or responding to threats he linked to Iran. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/2/18/trump-says-uks-starmer-big-mistake-diego-garcia-lease'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1974259/trump-warns-britain-not-to-give-away-military-base-at-diego-garcia'>Trump warns Britain not to ‘give away’ military base at Diego Garcia</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a hre